  "problem": "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-0.3)^{2}=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We need to calculate the product of $-0.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-0.3)^{2}=(-0.3)\\cdot (-0.3)=(0.3)\\cdot (0.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\times 3=9.\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($0.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($0.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $9$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{09}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-0.3)^{2}=0.09\\,.$",
